  "query_preview": "..\n\nTuesday and Tuesday night: The Inland NW will remain smokey but\nrelatively quiet one more day. A zonal/westerly flow will\npersist, but a trough starts to dig along the Pacific NW coast\nlater tonight. Expect continued breezy conditions near the\nCascades and a thermal axis near the Cascades that could enhance\nany fire growth there. Smoke from regional wildfires will continue\nto spread from west to east, still coming from the more active\nnear the Cascades and north-central WA. Temperatures will reach\nthe 80s to low 90s.  \n\nWednesday to Friday: A trough of low pressure will move into the\narea this period. This will come with increasing moisture and\nincreasing iinstability as the cooler mid-level temperatures \narrives with the upper trough. Models paint 100-500 J/kg over the \nCascades to northeast WA and north ID Wednesday afternoon. ECMWF \nlightning progs show a modest potential for thunderstorms across \nthe northern counties Wednesday afternoon, potentially \npropagating east and southeast across the Columbia Basin later in\nthe afternoon to evening. This will come with the risk for \nlightning caused fire starts and gusty outflow winds could also \nbring spread of any new or existing fires, with those outflows \nwinds more apt to expand a modest distance away from the parent \nt-storm. In addition, brief downpours and small hail will be \npossible with t-storms. The best chance for wetting rains will be \nin the northern counties. Confidence is low, but we will have to \nwatch for any debris flow with any downpours near some of these \nburn scars. Winds will increase from the north later Wednesday \ndown the Okanogan Valley to western Basin, creating a more generic\nelevated fire weather conditions. Temperatures will be in the 80s\nto low 90s, with some upper 70s Thursday as the coolest day.\n\nHeading into Thursday and Friday the area remains under the\nunstable upper trough. So isolated to scattered showers and a\nchance for t-storms will continue, though by this time largely\nnear the mountains. Some may slip across the northeast basin and\nPalouse too. Wind will remain breezy over central WA.\n\nSaturday to Monday: The Inland NW remains in a southwest flow and\nin the possible path of continues impulses/shortwaves. Most of\nthese are directed toward the southeast CWA, bringing some shower\nand isolated t-storm chances there this weekend. The risk expands\nout the more of the mountains Monday, possibly the lowlands. \nHowever these do not look like big precipitation producers. \nOverall confidence is low, but we'll have to watch any of this for\nany lightning impacts. Temperatures will be in the 80s to low \n90s. /Solveig",
